/* ============================================================================
   DB_SP_PRO / DB_SP_INF / DB_SP_SUP / DB_SP_WRH -- SCHEMA-ONLY MIGRATION
   Generated from "SP DB SYSTEM INFORMATION SCHEMA.xlsx" (PRO/INF/SUP sheets)
   and "DB_SP_WRH  SYSTEM INFORMATION SCHEMA.xlsx" (WRH sheet) -- raw
   INFORMATION_SCHEMA.COLUMNS exports, one row per column.

   Scope: every dbo-schema table in those four databases, empty (no data),
   columns only. Companion to DB_SP_SP-migration-candidates.sql, which
   covers DB_SP_SP and is NOT touched by this file.

   WHY COLUMNS-ONLY, NO CONSTRAINTS:
   INFORMATION_SCHEMA.COLUMNS (the only sheet available) carries no primary
   key, foreign key, identity, computed-column, index, or default-constraint
   metadata -- that lives in sys.columns/sys.key_constraints/sys.indexes,
   which weren't exported. Reproducing those from this data would mean
   guessing, so nothing here does. Every column is created exactly as
   introspected (type, length/precision/scale, nullability) and nothing
   more. This is enough for Sequelize to connect and read/write columns by
   name/type -- it is NOT a faithful clone of the source database's
   constraints, keys, or defaults. If a service's Sequelize model declares
   its own primary key or defines associations expecting FK enforcement,
   this migration does not provide that.

   IS_NULLABLE / DATA_TYPE / CHARACTER_MAXIMUM_LENGTH / NUMERIC_PRECISION /
   NUMERIC_SCALE / DATETIME_PRECISION are reproduced as-is from the dump.
   COLUMN_DEFAULT is deliberately NOT reproduced (raw default-constraint
   expressions in the export aren't safe to re-parse mechanically, and are
   moot for empty tables -- nothing is being inserted without an explicit
   value yet).

   EXCLUDED: the `cdc` schema (DB_SP_SUP, DB_SP_WRH) and `mdm` schema
   (DB_SP_INF) present in the raw dump -- SQL Server Change Data Capture /
   an MDM tool's own system tables, not application schema. Only `dbo` is
   created here.

   Idempotent: every CREATE DATABASE / CREATE TABLE is guarded so this is
   safe to re-run against a DB that already has some or all of this.
   ============================================================================ */

SET NOCOUNT ON;
GO


